    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d457">

                <pb facs="0802" n="670" xml:id="pg670"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Bulgaria)/6–3045:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 457<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy </hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State ad interim</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caserta</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-30T17:00:00+02:00">June 30, 1945—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2823. Gen Oxley head Brit Del ACC Bulgaria (our 2814, June 29<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d457-f1"/>) reports he is informed Russian division completed
                    crossing of Danube at Ruse about three days ago and moved southward towards
                    Trinova and Shumen. Oxley’s tentative estimate of Russian troops in Bulgar is
                    ten divs located roughly as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Two divs Varna Burgas; two divs Sliven Kotel area; one div between Yambol
                            and Turk frontier; one div Khaskovo Nomchilgrad; one div Plovdiv area;
                            one div along road south from Gorna Dzhumaya; probably at least two divs
                            north of Balkan Mountains. General opinion is that there are now 200,000
                            Soviet troops in Bulgar.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p> Oxley’s msg stated that in spite of doubtful veracity of many sources of info
                    today, above is probably fair picture of situation and it is impossible to
                    obtain any more accurate info.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
